The table structure information is (table names): 
['countries', 'customers', 'promotions', 'products', 'times', 'channels', 'sales', 'costs', 'supplementary_demographics', 'currency']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  

• countries.country_id : joins to customers.country_id so we can keep only the rows whose country = ‘Italy’.  
• countries.country_iso_code / countries.country_name : either of these can be used in the filter 'Italy'.  
• customers.cust_id : primary key of the customer table, required to join to sales.  
• sales.cust_id : join to customers.cust_id.  
• sales.time_id : join to times.time_id (to keep only December‑2021 rows) and to costs.time_id when we compute profit.  
• times.calendar_year / times.calendar_month_number : allow the “calendar_year = 2021 AND calendar_month_number = 12” filter.  
• sales.prod_id / sales.channel_id / sales.promo_id : together with sales.time_id they allow an exact join to costs for the profit computation.  
• costs.unit_cost : needed to compute profit = amount_sold – quantity_sold*unit_cost.  
• sales.quantity_sold / sales.amount_sold : the two parts of the profit formula.  

Main conditions that will be used  

1. countries.country_name = 'Italy'  (or countries.country_iso_code = 'IT')  
2. times.calendar_year = 2021  
3. times.calendar_month_number = 12  

Below are ten exploratory → final SQL queries (SQLite‑compatible).  
They are written in ascending order of complexity and each has a short explanation.  
All SELECTs are limited to 20 rows so you can inspect the data step‑by‑step.

```sql
-- 1. List the country rows that correspond to Italy
SELECT DISTINCT country_id, country_iso_code, country_name
FROM countries
WHERE country_name = 'Italy'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Show a few customers that belong to Italy
SELECT DISTINCT c.cust_id, c.cust_first_name, c.cust_last_name
FROM customers  AS c
JOIN countries  AS co  ON c.country_id = co.country_id
WHERE co.country_name = 'Italy'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Verify how the month of December‑2021 is stored in the times table
SELECT DISTINCT time_id, calendar_year, calendar_month_number, calendar_month_name
FROM times
WHERE calendar_year = 2021 AND calendar_month_number = 12
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Take a quick look at sales rows that happened in December‑2021
SELECT DISTINCT s.cust_id, s.prod_id, s.time_id, s.quantity_sold, s.amount_sold
FROM   sales AS s
JOIN   times AS t ON s.time_id = t.time_id
WHERE  t.calendar_year = 2021 AND t.calendar_month_number = 12
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join one sales row to its unit_cost to see how profit can be calculated
SELECT DISTINCT s.cust_id,
       s.prod_id,
       s.time_id,
       s.quantity_sold,
       s.amount_sold,
       c.unit_cost,
       (s.amount_sold - s.quantity_sold * c.unit_cost) AS profit
FROM   sales AS s
JOIN   costs AS c   ON  c.prod_id  = s.prod_id
                   AND c.time_id  = s.time_id
                   AND c.channel_id = s.channel_id
                   AND c.promo_id   = s.promo_id
JOIN   times AS t   ON t.time_id   = s.time_id
WHERE  t.calendar_year = 2021 AND t.calendar_month_number = 12
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Aggregate December‑2021 profit per Italian customer
SELECT DISTINCT cu.cust_id,
       SUM(s.amount_sold - s.quantity_sold * co.unit_cost) AS dec2021_profit
FROM   customers AS cu
JOIN   countries AS ctr ON ctr.country_id = cu.country_id
JOIN   sales     AS s   ON s.cust_id     = cu.cust_id
JOIN   costs     AS co  ON co.prod_id    = s.prod_id
                       AND co.time_id    = s.time_id
                       AND co.channel_id = s.channel_id
                       AND co.promo_id   = s.promo_id
JOIN   times     AS ti  ON ti.time_id    = s.time_id
WHERE  ctr.country_name = 'Italy'
  AND  ti.calendar_year = 2021
  AND  ti.calendar_month_number = 12
GROUP  BY cu.cust_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. How many Italian customers actually bought something in December‑2021?
SELECT COUNT(*) AS italian_customers_with_sales_in_dec2021
FROM (
  SELECT cu.cust_id
  FROM   customers AS cu
  JOIN   countries AS ctr ON ctr.country_id = cu.country_id
  JOIN   sales     AS s   ON s.cust_id     = cu.cust_id
  JOIN   times     AS ti  ON ti.time_id    = s.time_id
  WHERE  ctr.country_name = 'Italy'
    AND  ti.calendar_year = 2021
    AND  ti.calendar_month_number = 12
  GROUP  BY cu.cust_id
);
```

```sql
-- 8. Attach a decile (NTILE(10)) to each Italian customer’s total profit for Dec‑2021
WITH per_cust AS (
  SELECT cu.cust_id,
         SUM(s.amount_sold - s.quantity_sold * co.unit_cost) AS dec2021_profit
  FROM   customers AS cu
  JOIN   countries AS ctr ON ctr.country_id = cu.country_id
  JOIN   sales     AS s   ON s.cust_id     = cu.cust_id
  JOIN   costs     AS co  ON co.prod_id    = s.prod_id
                         AND co.time_id    = s.time_id
                         AND co.channel_id = s.channel_id
                         AND co.promo_id   = s.promo_id
  JOIN   times     AS ti  ON ti.time_id    = s.time_id
  WHERE  ctr.country_name = 'Italy'
    AND  ti.calendar_year = 2021
    AND  ti.calendar_month_number = 12
  GROUP  BY cu.cust_id
)
SELECT DISTINCT cust_id,
       dec2021_profit,
       NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY dec2021_profit) AS profit_decile
FROM   per_cust
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Show min & max profit inside each decile
WITH per_cust AS (
  SELECT cu.cust_id,
         SUM(s.amount_sold - s.quantity_sold * co.unit_cost) AS dec2021_profit
  FROM   customers AS cu
  JOIN   countries AS ctr ON ctr.country_id = cu.country_id
  JOIN   sales     AS s   ON s.cust_id     = cu.cust_id
  JOIN   costs     AS co  ON co.prod_id    = s.prod_id
                         AND co.time_id    = s.time_id
                         AND co.channel_id = s.channel_id
                         AND co.promo_id   = s.promo_id
  JOIN   times     AS ti  ON ti.time_id    = s.time_id
  WHERE  ctr.country_name = 'Italy'
    AND  ti.calendar_year = 2021
    AND  ti.calendar_month_number = 12
  GROUP  BY cu.cust_id
),
deciled AS (
  SELECT cust_id,
         dec2021_profit,
         NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY dec2021_profit) AS profit_decile
  FROM   per_cust
)
SELECT DISTINCT profit_decile,
       MIN(dec2021_profit) AS lowest_profit,
       MAX(dec2021_profit) AS highest_profit
FROM   deciled
GROUP  BY profit_decile
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: min & max profit for each of the 10 evenly‑sized tiers (deciles)
WITH per_cust AS (
  SELECT cu.cust_id,
         SUM(s.amount_sold - s.quantity_sold * co.unit_cost) AS dec2021_profit
  FROM   customers AS cu
  JOIN   countries AS ctr ON ctr.country_id = cu.country_id
  JOIN   sales     AS s   ON s.cust_id     = cu.cust_id
  JOIN   costs     AS co  ON co.prod_id    = s.prod_id
                         AND co.time_id    = s.time_id
                         AND co.channel_id = s.channel_id
                         AND co.promo_id   = s.promo_id
  JOIN   times     AS ti  ON ti.time_id    = s.time_id
  WHERE  ctr.country_name = 'Italy'
    AND  ti.calendar_year = 2021
    AND  ti.calendar_month_number = 12
  GROUP  BY cu.cust_id
),
deciled AS (
  SELECT cust_id,
         dec2021_profit,
         NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY dec2021_profit) AS profit_decile
  FROM   per_cust
)
SELECT profit_decile                                   AS tier,
       MIN(dec2021_profit) AS lowest_profit_in_tier,
       MAX(dec2021_profit) AS highest_profit_in_tier
FROM   deciled
GROUP  BY profit_decile
ORDER  BY profit_decile
LIMIT 20;
```

